      "name": "complaint_scope",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Whether a complaint targets the whole product vs a feature is a useful discriminating signal. Mostly grounded, but AMZ-0002 labeled 'specific_use_case' is a weak fit (the complaint is about book content/political commentary, a feature-level issue), and AMZ-0001 'entire_product' is debatable given it's an availability complaint."
    },
    {
      "name": "return_action_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Behavioral intent (returns, warns others, refund) is a high-value predictor of dissatisfaction. Sampled values are conservative and well-grounded, correctly using not_present/no_action_mentioned where text is silent."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.83,
  "source_groundedness": 0.7,
  "rationale": "All five columns provide distinct, non-trivial axes (reason, severity gap, intensity, scope, behavioral signal) directly relevant to predicting negative satisfaction, with no near-constant or redundant restatement issues. Groundedness is generally sound with correct 'not_present'/NULL handling for silent rows, but several sampled rows (notably AMZ-0001, AMZ-0002) show over-confident category assignments—especially 'misleading_claim' and specific scope/intensity labels—not clearly warranted by the source text, indicating moderate hallucination risk."
